AB  - Believable Non-Player Character (NPC) voices are critical for creating immersive experiences in Virtual Reality (VR). However, traditional voice acting is costly and lacks the scalability for extensive dialogues and localization, particularly for languages like Brazilian Portuguese. While Text-to-Speech (TTS) offers a viable alternative, there is a lack of systematic evaluation of modern models’ perceptual naturalness when generating domain-specific NPC dialogues in Portuguese. This paper addresses this gap by conducting a subjective evaluation of five State-of-the-Art TTS models: XTTS, Parler TTS, F5-TTS, Fish Speech, and Orpheus. We created two test sets by translating dialogues from the LIGHT (narrative role-play) and CHiME-6 (spontaneous conversation) datasets. The naturalness of the synthesized audio was assessed through a Mean Opinion Score (MOS) user study with 13 native speakers. Our results indicate a moderate overall naturalness (average MOS = 3.29), with Parler TTS and XTTS emerging as the top performers, particularly with conversational text (MOS = 3.53). These findings provide a crucial benchmark for developers creating localized VR content and highlight that achieving truly expressive NPC voices remains a significant research challenge.
ER  - 

