AB  - Parkinson’s Disease (PD) impairs motor function and limits upper-limb rehabilitation. This work employs a conditional Generative Adversarial Network (cGAN) to generate realistic 3D motion trajectories from Haptic-VR data. A Dynamic Time Warping (DTW)-based validation framework uses ROC analysis and Youden’s J statistic to determine optimal thresholds for filtering synthetic samples. Results show that generated trajectories preserve overall motion structure but exhibit reduced variability. Classification experiments using SVM, Random Forest, and Logistic Regression show gesturedependent performance changes, with improvements in some cases and minor degradation in others. These findings demonstrate the potential of GAN-based augmentation for rehabilitation while highlighting the need to improve trajectory diversity.
