AB  - This study explores the combination of large-scale multimodal models and augmented reality technology to build a real-time graphical and textual interaction system. The BLIP model is adopted to achieve visual positioning, GPT-4V is responsible for language reasoning, and a dual-channel architecture is designed to process AR image streams and natural language queries in real time. By constructing three types of AR data, namely indoor static environment, outdoor navigation scene, and museum education, the system's performance in visual question answering and cross-modal content generation is evaluated. The actual measurement shows that the accuracy rate of visual question answering of this scheme reaches 83.5%, and the FID score of image generation is 27.3, surpassing the baseline models based on CLIP and GPT-3. The ablation experiment confirmed that the unique gaze tracking and scene mapping of AR play a key role in improving the correlation of results. Despite network dependency and hardware limitations, the system response time was controlled within 250 milliseconds, and user experience satisfaction remained consistently high. These results indicate that the deep integration of multimodal models and AR environments will significantly expand the boundaries of human-computer collaboration and open new avenues for educational assistance, virtual tours, and cultural heritage exhibitions.
ER  - 

