AB  - The digitization of cultural artifacts has evolved from passive reproduction to active cultural symbiosis, yet rigorous quantitative evaluation of the underlying display systems remains insufficient. This study proposes and evaluates a technology-integrated digital artifact display framework combining Generative Adversarial Network (GAN)-based image restoration, photogrammetry-driven 3D modeling, and an immersive Virtual Reality (VR) presentation interface. An empirical experiment was conducted with 120 participants, assessing image quality using Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR) and Structural Similarity Index (SSIM), and measuring user experience via a standardized questionnaire. The proposed GAN model achieved an average PSNR of 28.45 dB and SSIM of 0.89, outperforming bicubic interpolation by 18.0% and 18.7% respectively. User immersion scores increased by 35.5% and knowledge transfer improved by 42.1% compared to conventional 2D displays. These results confirm the feasibility and cultural communication value of the proposed system, offering a framework for digital heritage institutions.
ER  - 

