AB  - The COVID-19 pandemic accelerated the shift of professional interactions meetings, collaborations, and workplace engagements into virtual environments. As people increasingly represented themselves through avatars, limited customization options undermined authentic self-representation, particularly for individuals from diverse ethnic backgrounds, reducing the sense of embodiment and inclusion. This gap motivated the present study, which investigates how workers perceive and respond to virtual colleagues varying in ethnicity, gender, and age within simulated professional settings. To enable this inquiry, we developed an end-to-end generative pipeline converting natural language prompts into customizable, animatable 3D humanoid avatars using text-to-image synthesis, TRELLIS-based 3D reconstruction, and automated skeletal rigging via Adobe Mixamo. An exploratory user study (N = 25) examined representational patterns and attitudinal responses across ten workplace scenarios. Descriptive analyses revealed scenario-conditioned clustering of perceived ethnicity and variation in collaboration willingness. A Scheirer–Ray–Hare test identified a significant main effect of professional scenario on collaboration intent, while perceived ethnicity alone did not reach significance. These findings suggest contextual role framing shapes collaboration responses more strongly than ethnicity, highlighting the need to jointly examine generative AI representation and human perception in fair, inclusive virtual workplaces.
ER  - 

